DOM, CSSOM and Render Tree (Easy Explanation)

1. DOM (What exists on the page)
- Browser reads all HTML (head + body).
- Turns every tag into a tree (like a family tree).
- DOM = “These elements exist.”

Example:
<body>
  <h1>Hello</h1>
  <p>World</p>
</body>

DOM says:
There is a body → inside it h1 → inside it text “Hello” → then a p → inside it “World”.

2. CSSOM ((CSS Object Model) How things should look)
- Browser reads all CSS rules.
- Builds another tree that stores the styles.
- CSSOM = “How these elements should look.”

Example:
h1 { color: red; }
p { font-size: 20px; }

CSSOM says:
h1 should be red.
p should be 20px.

3. Render Tree (What will actually be visible on screen)
- Browser combines DOM + CSSOM.
- Removes elements that should not be shown (like display:none).
- Render Tree = “What will appear on the screen.”

Example:
If a paragraph has display:none → it will NOT exist in the render tree.

4. Final Flow
HTML → DOM  
CSS → CSSOM  
DOM + CSSOM → Render Tree  
Render Tree → Layout (positions)  
Layout → Paint (pixels on screen)
